Here's my review. I found **3 actionable problems** and have notes on the two skipped tests.

---

## 1. Sleepy Test — `Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes` (line 62)

**Severity: High**

`Thread.Sleep(3000)` adds 3 seconds of wall-clock time to every run and is inherently racy — the delay might be too short on a loaded CI agent, or wastefully long otherwise. This makes the suite slow and flaky, both of which erode trust in CI.

**Fix:** Wait on the actual async signal instead of guessing a duration.

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    await service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");

    Assert.True(service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"));
}
```

If `ReplenishAsync` returns `void` and can't be changed, wrap the poll in a bounded retry loop with a short interval and a timeout, rather than a single blind sleep.

---

## 2. Assertion-Free Test — `Audit_RunsWithoutError` (line 75)

**Severity: High**

This test calls `service.Audit()` and never asserts anything. It will pass even if `Audit()` silently corrupts state or returns wrong data — the only failure mode is an unhandled exception. A test that can't detect regressions is worse than no test, because it inflates code-coverage numbers and gives false confidence.

**Fix:** Assert the observable outcome of the audit.

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Audit_RunsWithoutError()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    var result = service.Audit();

    Assert.NotNull(result);
    Assert.Equal(1, result.TotalItems);   // adjust to the real return type
}
```

If `Audit()` truly returns `void` and only has side-effects, assert on those side-effects (e.g., an audit log entry was created).

---

## 3. Bare Skip — `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` (line 50)

**Severity: Medium**

The skip reason is just `"skip"`. Nobody looking at this later can tell whether the feature is blocked, broken, or obsolete. Skipped tests that lack justification tend to stay skipped forever, hiding dead code or missing coverage. This is medium rather than high because the test *itself* is well-structured — the problem is purely the unexplained disable.

**Fix:** Document the reason and link a tracking item, or delete the test.

```csharp
[Fact(Skip = "Tracked by #XXXX — Restock API not yet implemented")]
public void Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities()
{
    // ...
}
```

---

## Notes on the two skipped tests

| Test | How worried? |
|---|---|
| `Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock` (line 37) | **Low concern.** The skip message names a tracking issue (`#1487`) and explains the blocker (warehouse API redesign). This is the right way to disable a test — the only action item is to periodically check whether #1487 has been resolved. |
| `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` (line 50) | **Medium concern.** Covered above — no rationale, no issue link. This should be fixed or removed before it becomes permanent dead weight. |

---

### Not flagged

`AddItems_ThreeAdded_CountIsThree` and `RemoveItem_FromTwo_LeavesOne` use small integer literals (`3`, `1`, `2`) that are self-evident from the test setup. These are not magic numbers — the values directly correspond to the items being added/removed. No issue here.
